## Appended by Stage 2a-2, 2026-09-12: the empirical arm becomes reproducible

The empirical arm previously read `dct_realeccs_trimmed.json`, a 2026-03 EC3
pull stored POST-cleaning. It is superseded but not deleted: it is the record of
the data the current manuscript reports, and the rows above still pin it.

`data/raw/ec3_raw_ecc_2026-08-14.csv.gz` replaces it as the input. It is RAW,
which is the point: no outlier rule has been applied to it, so the cleaning rule
can be applied symmetrically. One row per EPD, with the provenance needed to
audit any value. Its `_runmeta.json` records the query, the pull dates, the
source checksum and the ECC definition.

**It is not an API pull, and it could not be.** The EC3 API returns HTTP 403 for
this account, "Direct API access is not allowed for private or restricted
accounts". The key is recognized; the account permission is not. The file is a
slice of the consolidated EPD store at `../EPDsFromEC3/store`, whose 138-category
records were pulled on 2026-08-13 and 2026-08-14 through the correctly-
paginating LucidLCA wrapper: five months newer than the 2026-03 data, and raw.
Restoring API access and re-pulling is an open item; see
`reports/HANDOFF_stage-2a2.md`.

Freezing it here is what makes the empirical arm reproducible at all. An API
pull is not reproducible by a reader even when it works, because EC3's contents
change; an archived, checksummed extract is.

